</tbody>
</table></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel">
<li id="S4.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">†</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Effective NFE at a resolution of <math id="S4.I1.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32\times 32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>32</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32\times 32</annotation></semantics></math> obtained by converting the low-resolution NFEs using a latency-based scaling factor.</p>
</div></li>
</ul></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate our model on four benchmark datasets: (1) Unconditional CIFAR-10 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>: 50,000 training images of size <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32\times 32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>32</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32\times 32</annotation></semantics></math> across 10 classes, without class conditioning. (2) Conditional CIFAR-10 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>: The same dataset but with class labels used as additional conditioning inputs. (3) FFHQ64<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times 64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times 64</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>: 70,000 high-quality human face images downsampled to <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="64\times 64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>64</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64\times 64</annotation></semantics></math> resolution, used in the unconditional setting. (4) ImageNet256<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times 64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times 64</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>: A large-scale dataset consisting of over 1.2 million training images from 1,000 object categories, with all images resized to <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="256\times 256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>256</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256\times 256</annotation></semantics></math> resolution. It is commonly used in the conditional setting, where class labels serve as conditioning inputs.
We use two widely adopted metrics for numerical evaluation: Fréchet Inception Distance (FID) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> which measures the distance between the feature distributions of generated and real images, and Inception Score (IS) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>, which evaluates the quality and diversity of generated images based on classifier confidence.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Following the training procedure described in Section <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 Training and Sampling with the Unified Architecture ‣ 3 Methods ‣ LowDiff: Efficient Diffusion Sampling with Low-Resolution Condition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>, the number of activated output resolutions is dynamically determined by the target dataset resolution. When training on CIFAR-10 (the resolution is <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32\times 32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>32</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32\times 32</annotation></semantics></math>), the model includes three output resolution stages: <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8\times 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8\times 8</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16\times 16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16\times 16</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32\times 32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>32</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32\times 32</annotation></semantics></math>. When training on FFHQ64<math id="S4.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>64 (the resolution is <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="64\times 64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>64</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64\times 64</annotation></semantics></math>), the model includes four output resolution stages: <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8\times 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8\times 8</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16\times 16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16\times 16</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32\times 32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>32</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32\times 32</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="64\times 64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>64</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64\times 64</annotation></semantics></math>. When training on ImageNet256<math id="S4.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>256 (the original latent space is <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16\times 16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16\times 16</annotation></semantics></math>), the model includes two output resolution stages: <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8\times 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8\times 8</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16\times 16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16\times 16</annotation></semantics></math>. All models are trained on 8 NVIDIA A40 GPUs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Results</h3>

